{"name":"White Rabbit","hex":"#f8eee7","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"white rabbit","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Interjection","definitions":["A phrase said, before saying anything else, on the first day of the month in order to bring good luck."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Someone or something which moves rapidly or hurriedly from place to place."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/white_rabbit"}],"entries":[{"title":"White Rabbit","blocks":[{"text":"The White Rabbit is a fictional anthropomorphic rabbit who appears as a key character in Lewis Carroll's 1865 book Alice's Adventures in Wonderland. He is depicted as a hurried, waistcoat-clad lagomorph who perpetually consults his pocket watch and mutters of his tardiness as he scurries about in his duties. His appearance and behaviour incite the protagonist Alice to pursue him down a rabbit hole, initiating her journey into Wonderland. Alice encounters him again when he mistakes her for his housemaid Mary Ann and she becomes trapped in his house after growing too large. In the last few chapters, he appears as a herald and footman to the King and Queen of Hearts. The White Rabbit has been depicted in numerous adaptations, including Disney's 1951 and 2010 films."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ec/Down_the_Rabbit_Hole.png/330px-Down_the_Rabbit_Hole.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/White_Rabbit"},{"title":"White Rabbit (song)","blocks":[{"text":"\"White Rabbit\" is a song written by Grace Slick and recorded by the American rock band Jefferson Airplane for their 1967 album Surrealistic Pillow. It draws on imagery from Lewis Carroll's 1865 book Alice's Adventures in Wonderland and its 1871 sequel Through the Looking-Glass."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/5/53/White_Rabbit_label.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/White_Rabbit_(song)"},{"title":"Rabbit","blocks":[{"text":"Rabbits, or bunnies, are small mammals in the family Leporidae, which is in the order Lagomorpha. They are familiar throughout the world as a small herbivore, a prey animal, a domesticated form of livestock, and a pet, having a widespread effect on ecologies and cultures. The most widespread rabbit genera are Oryctolagus and Sylvilagus. The former, Oryctolagus, includes the European rabbit, Oryctolagus cuniculus, which is the ancestor of the hundreds of breeds of domestic rabbit and has been introduced on every continent except Antarctica. The latter, Sylvilagus, includes over 13 wild rabbit species, among them the cottontails and tapetis. Wild rabbits not included in Oryctolagus and Sylvilagus include several species of limited distribution, including the pygmy rabbit, volcano rabbit, and Sumatran striped rabbit."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1f/Oryctolagus_cuniculus_Rcdo.jpg/330px-Oryctolagus_cuniculus_Rcdo.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rabbit"},{"title":"White","blocks":[{"text":"White is the lightest color and is achromatic. It is the color of objects such as snow, chalk, and milk, and is the opposite of black. White objects fully reflect and scatter all the visible wavelengths of light. White on television and computer screens is created by a mixture of red, blue, and green light. The color white can be given with white pigments, especially titanium dioxide."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/Delphinapterus_leucas_2.jpg/330px-Delphinapterus_leucas_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/White"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/8/8f/Alice_in_Wonderland_pg41_-_Alice_meets_the_White_Rabbit_-_by_Margaret_Winifred_Tarrant_1916.jpg/250px-Alice_in_Wonderland_pg41_-_Alice_meets_the_White_Rabbit_-_by_Margaret_Winifred_Tarrant_1916.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/8/8f/Alice_in_Wonderland_pg41_-_Alice_meets_the_White_Rabbit_-_by_Margaret_Winifred_Tarrant_1916.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/8/8f/Alice_in_Wonderland_pg41_-_Alice_meets_the_White_Rabbit_-_by_Margaret_Winifred_Tarrant_1916.jpg/250px-Alice_in_Wonderland_pg41_-_Alice_meets_the_White_Rabbit_-_by_Margaret_Winifred_Tarrant_1916.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Alice meeting the Rabbit","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAlice_in_Wonderland_pg41_-_Alice_meets_the_White_Rabbit_-_by_Margaret_Winifred_Tarrant_1916.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/Alice_in_Wonderland_%281951%29_-_White_Rabbit.png/500px-Alice_in_Wonderland_%281951%29_-_White_Rabbit.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/Alice_in_Wonderland_%281951%29_-_White_Rabbit.png/1280px-Alice_in_Wonderland_%281951%29_-_White_Rabbit.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/Alice_in_Wonderland_%281951%29_-_White_Rabbit.png/500px-Alice_in_Wonderland_%281951%29_-_White_Rabbit.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAlice_in_Wonderland_(1951)_-_White_Rabbit.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/d/d2/WhiteRabbit.jpg/250px-WhiteRabbit.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/d/d2/WhiteRabbit.jpg?utm_sour